> word.

My bad.  I was confusing what are strictly called possesive adjectives 
and posessive pronouns:

1st person singular	I have my ticket.	I have mine.
2nd person singular	You have your book.	You have yours.
3rd person singular	She has her lunch.	She has hers.
3rd person singular	He has his glove.	He has his.
1st person plural	We have our boat.	We have ours.
2nd person plural	You have your jacket.	You have yours.
3rd person plural	They have their cats.	They have theirs.

The 3rd person singular male case uses the same word for both (his).

> You could say "its book" but not "hers book". In the latter case, it
> should be "her book", which would make the example unintelligable. Also,
> you could say "the book is hers", but not "the book is its".

Well, it isn't much used, but I think you can. "See that tree? The leaf 
is its."


